{%- comment -%}
  PDP gallery presentation.

  Adds no gallery of its own. The desktop two-column grid, the media rendering,
  video with its play button, zoom and the product.media ordering all stay with
  Prestige's own product-gallery snippet - the grid comes from its "Grid (2 per
  row)" layout, which already emits repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)) with a
  configurable gap. This file only adjusts presentation that no setting exposes:

    - the gallery column width on desktop
    - one media plus a sliver of the next on mobile, one item per swipe
    - the arrow treatment used across the homepage carousels

  Nothing in snippets/product-gallery.liquid, theme.css or theme.js is touched,
  so the Default product template and every other template are unaffected.

  The product section is found by its own class, shopify-section--main-product,
  which main-product.liquid declares in its schema - so nothing has to be
  configured per product or per template. The rules reach only that section, and
  only where this CSS section has been added, which is the product template.

  Add this section to the product template, anywhere - it renders no markup and
  only emits scoped CSS.
{%- endcomment -%}

<style>
  {%- comment -%}
    ---------- Desktop ----------
    The column is sized by the section's own "Product info width" setting, so
    the width is not restated here - that would fight the setting and drift
    from it. Only the gap is pinned, because the design calls for the same
    16px horizontally and vertically and the theme's own setting drives both.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  @media screen and (min-width: 1000px) {
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__carousel {
      --product-gallery-carousel-gap: {{ section.settings.grid_gap }}px;
    }

    {%- comment -%}
      Each cell keeps the design's proportion rather than the source image's,
      so a portrait and a landscape photograph sit in a tidy grid. object-fit
      crops rather than distorting - rugs must never be stretched.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if section.settings.lock_ratio -%}
      .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__media {
        aspect-ratio: {{ section.settings.media_ratio }};
        overflow: hidden;
      }

      .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__media :is(img, video, iframe, model-viewer) {
        width: 100%;
        height: 100%;
        object-fit: cover;
      }
    {%- endif -%}
  }

  {%- comment -%}
    ---------- Product information column ----------
    Desktop only. Prestige builds the product grid from a percentage of the
    container, which cannot express the design's 537px exactly: at a 1300px
    column with a 32px gap the setting's 1% step lands on 532 or 545. The
    columns are stated directly instead - the information column is a fixed
    width and the gallery takes what is left, so the two always add up to the
    container and the gap is exactly the design value.

    grid-template-columns after the theme's `grid` shorthand overrides only the
    columns; the areas and rows the shorthand set are untouched. Specificity is
    (0,2,0) against the theme's (0,1,0), so no !important is needed.

    Inside a min-width query, so below 1000px Prestige's own stacked layout is
    left completely alone and no fixed width can overflow a phone.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  @media screen and (min-width: 1000px) {
    .shopify-section--main-product .product {
      --product-gap: {{ section.settings.info_gap }}px;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, {{ section.settings.info_width }}px);
    }
  }

  {%- comment -%}
    ---------- Mobile order ----------
    Information above the gallery below 1000px.

    main-product builds the stacked layout as grid areas -
    "product-gallery" "product-info" - so the gallery comes first simply
    because it is named first. Re-stating the areas in the other order moves
    the whole column without touching the markup: the information is still
    rendered once, in one place, and nothing is duplicated or moved by script.

    product-content is kept in the list because the section adds that area when
    there are blocks below the gallery. An area with no item in it collapses to
    a zero-height track, so naming it is safe whether or not it exists.

    Desktop is untouched: its two-column areas are declared inside the
    section's own min-width query and this rule cannot reach them.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  @media screen and (max-width: 999px) {
    .shopify-section--main-product .product {
      display: block;
    }

    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery,
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-group {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }

    /* price in the first track, savings in the second - auto-placement keeps
       them on one row because the pill follows the price */
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item[data-block-type='price'] {
      grid-column: 1;
      justify-self: start;
    }

    .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item:has(.pdp-savings) {
      grid-column: 2;
      justify-self: start;
      align-self: center;
      margin-inline-start: {{ section.settings.price_gap }}px;
    }

    .shopify-section--main-product .product-info,
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-list {
      display: contents;
    }

    /* tighter rhythm on a narrow column */
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-info {
      --product-info-gap: {{ section.settings.info_row_gap_mobile }}px;
    }

    /* 1 - information, above the gallery */
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item {
      order: 1;
    }

    /* 2 - the gallery */
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery {
      order: 2;
    }

    /* 3 - everything to do with configuring and buying, below the gallery.
       Listed by block type, so a block type not named here stays with the
       information group by default. */
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item:is(
      [data-block-type='variant-picker'],
      [data-block-type='quantity-selector'],
      [data-block-type='buy-buttons'],
      [data-block-type='line-item-property'],
      [data-block-type='pickup-availability'],
      [data-block-type='payment-terms'],
      [data-block-type='inventory'],
      [data-block-type='offers'],
      [data-block-type='volume-pricing'],
      [data-block-type='complementary-products'],
      [data-block-type='product-variations']
    ),
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-group {
      order: 3;
    }
  }

  {%- comment -%}
    ---------- Pill ----------
    Shape and spacing only. The value comes from the pill snippet, which reuses
    the product card's own expression.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Prestige stacks the information blocks with --product-info-gap, 1rem by
    default - that is the space under the pill, the title, the rating and the
    description. Reset once here rather than per block, so the rhythm stays
    even and one setting moves them together.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .shopify-section--main-product .product-info {
    --product-info-gap: {{ section.settings.info_row_gap }}px;
  }
 .product-info__block-item:is([data-block-type=variant-picker],[data-block-type=product-variations],[data-block-type=separator],[data-block-type=description],[data-block-type=text],[data-block-type=liquid],[data-block-type=share-buttons])
  {
   --product-info-gap: {{ section.settings.info_row_gap }}px;
  }
  {%- comment -%}
    Rating row: stars, value and the linked review count on one line. The count
    is underlined so it reads as the link it is; the stars and value are the
    theme's own markup and are not restyled here.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The stars and the two text runs are separate inline-flex boxes, each with
    its own line box, so centring the row still leaves them a pixel or two
    apart. Every part is given the same line-height and aligned on the same
    axis instead: the row centres its children, and each child centres its own
    contents, so the star glyphs and the text share one optical line.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .shopify-section--main-product .pdp-rating {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0 {{ section.settings.rating_gap }}px;
  }

  .shopify-section--main-product .pdp-rating .rating-badge,
  .shopify-section--main-product .pdp-rating .rating-badge__stars,
  .shopify-section--main-product .pdp-rating__link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    line-height: {{ section.settings.rating_line_height }}px;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* an svg is replaced content and sits on the text baseline by default, which
     is what drops the stars below the numbers */
  .shopify-section--main-product .pdp-rating .rating-badge__stars > svg {
    display: block;
    vertical-align: middle;
  }

  {%- comment -%}
    Type is stated once, outside any media query: the design uses the same
    values at every width, so a mobile override would only be a second place to
    keep in step.

    The family is var(--text-font-family), which is the theme's configured body
    font, rather than naming Figtree directly - the theme loads that font and
    serves it with the right fallbacks, and naming it here would break silently
    if a weight were not loaded. If the body font is ever changed away from
    Figtree, this line is where to pin it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .shopify-section--main-product .pdp-rating,
  .shopify-section--main-product .pdp-rating .rating-badge {
    font-family: var(--text-font-family);
    font-size: {{ section.settings.rating_font_size }}px;
    line-height: {{ section.settings.rating_line_height }}px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: #000000;
  }

  .shopify-section--main-product .pdp-rating .rating-badge__stars > svg {
    width: {{ section.settings.rating_star_size }}px;
    height: {{ section.settings.rating_star_size }}px;
  }

  {%- comment -%}
    theme.css gives the rating text its own smaller size through .text-xxs and
    a subdued colour; both are reset so the row reads at the design's size and
    in black.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .shopify-section--main-product .pdp-rating :is(.text-xxs, .text-subdued) {
    font-size: inherit;
    line-height: inherit;
    color: inherit;
  }

  .shopify-section--main-product .pdp-rating__link {
    color: #000000;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .shopify-section--main-product .pdp-pills {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-block-end: {{ section.settings.pill_gap }}px;
  }

  .shopify-section--main-product .pdp-pill {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 6px 14px;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 100px;
    background: {{ section.settings.pill_background }};
    color: rgb(var(--text-color));
    font-family: var(--text-font-family);
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.4;
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  {%- comment -%}
    ---------- Description and price ----------
    The description block renders the product's own rich text, so the bullets
    come from the product content in Shopify, not from the theme. The list
    markers are removed and each item kept as its own line with a dot leading
    it, which is the design's presentation of the same dynamic content - no
    text is rewritten here and nothing is hardcoded.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item[data-block-type='description'] :is(ul, ol) {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  {%- comment -%}
    The points run on as continuous text rather than one per row, so the
    description fills the column and wraps naturally over a couple of lines.
    Each item is inline and a dot is inserted BETWEEN items - li + li, so the
    first point never starts with one and a product with a single point shows
    no separator at all.

    The separator is presentation, not content: the product's own text is
    untouched, and a screen reader still reads the items as a list.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item[data-block-type='description'] li {
    display: inline;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item[data-block-type='description'] li + li::before {
    content: "\00B7";
    margin-inline: {{ section.settings.description_line_gap }}px;
  }

  {%- comment -%}
    Only the first couple of lines are shown. The clamp counts rendered lines
    rather than characters, so it holds at any column width and in any
    language - 537px of desktop and 351px of mobile each fill exactly the
    number of lines set here and end with an ellipsis. The product's full text
    is untouched and still present for search engines and screen readers.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Only the first few points are shown. A count of POINTS, not of rendered
    lines: a line clamp cuts wherever the column happens to run out, mid-word,
    while this always ends on a complete sentence whatever the width.

    The rest stay in the DOM rather than being deleted, so the full description
    is still there for search engines and screen readers - and raising the
    setting brings them back with no code change.

    Any heading inside the description is hidden too. Products here begin their
    description with a "Description" heading of their own, which is content
    rather than theme markup; this removes it from the PDP without editing
    every product. If a product's description legitimately needs a visible
    heading, turn this off and remove the word from the product instead.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item[data-block-type='description'] li:nth-child(n + {{ section.settings.description_points | plus: 1 }}) {
    display: none;
  }

  .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item[data-block-type='description'] .prose > :is(h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6) {
    display: none;
  }

  {%- comment -%}
    Price, compare-at and the savings pill on one row. The savings pill is a
    separate Liquid block, so it is a sibling of the price block rather than a
    child - the two are pulled onto one line here instead of by restructuring
    the blocks.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The price and the savings pill are two separate blocks, so each is its own
    row in the information column and the pill drops below the prices however
    the price block itself is styled.

    The two are put on one row by taking the price block out of the box tree
    with display: contents and letting the column lay out their children
    directly, then giving the price list and the pill the same flex line. The
    block list is a grid, so the row is created with a two-column track sized to
    content - the pill sits beside the prices and the row still wraps to the
    column width rather than overflowing it.

    No markup change, no reordering, no JavaScript.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item[data-block-type='price'] {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0 {{ section.settings.price_gap }}px;
  }

  .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item[data-block-type='price'] .price-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0 {{ section.settings.price_gap }}px;
  }
  .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item[data-block-type='price'] compare-at-price .price-list .text-subdued{
    color:#858585;
  }
  {%- comment -%}
    product-info__block-list is a plain block container - its children are
    block-level and stacked with margins, which is why the pill starts a new
    row however the price block itself is styled.

    Making both blocks inline-level puts them on the same line, with their own
    vertical margins zeroed so the line box is not inflated. Spacing above and
    below is unaffected: the block before still contributes margin-block-end
    and the block after still contributes margin-block-start.

    No negative margins, no absolute positioning, no markup change.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item[data-block-type='price'],
  .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item:has(.pdp-savings) {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    vertical-align: baseline;
    margin-block: 0;
  }

  .shopify-section--main-product .product-info__block-item:has(.pdp-savings) {
    margin-inline-start: {{ section.settings.price_gap }}px;
  }

  .shopify-section--main-product .pdp-savings {
    align-self: center;
  }

  {%- comment -%}
    ---------- Badge ----------
    .badge-list is only a flex row in theme.css - the product card positions it
    by making its own figure the containing block. The gallery media has no
    positioning context, so the badge would sit in normal flow above the image
    and push it down. Both breakpoints, since the badge belongs to the first
    media item on desktop and mobile alike.

    The badge's own colours and text come from product-badges.liquid and the
    theme's badge styles, exactly as on the homepage card - only the position
    is set here.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__media {
    position: relative;
  }

  .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__media > .pdp-media-badges {
    z-index: 1;
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: {{ section.settings.badge_top }}px;
    inset-inline-start: {{ section.settings.badge_left }}px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 4px;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* the lists inside the wrapper stay in normal flow - the wrapper is the one
     positioned element, so several badges stack instead of overlapping */
  .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__media .pdp-media-badges .badge-list {
    position: static;
  }

  .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__media > .badge-list {
    z-index: 1;
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: {{ section.settings.badge_top }}px;
    inset-inline-start: {{ section.settings.badge_left }}px;
    /* out of the flow, so it cannot change the media's size or the grid */
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  {%- comment -%}
    ---------- Mobile ----------
    One media item plus a sliver of the next, one item per swipe.

    The same shape as the homepage carousels: the track spans the viewport, the
    start inset is padding on the track and the end inset is a margin on the
    LAST cell - never padding-inline-end, which several engines drop from
    scrollWidth and which then clips the final item. scroll-padding matches the
    start inset so the first snap position resolves to 0.

    Widths are percentages of the track, so nothing here can widen the page:
    no 100vw, no negative page margins, no fixed pixel widths.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  @media screen and (max-width: 999px) {
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__carousel {
      --product-gallery-carousel-grid: auto / auto-flow {{ section.settings.mobile_media_width }}%;
      --product-gallery-carousel-gap: {{ section.settings.mobile_gap }}px;
      --product-gallery-carousel-scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
      scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;     
      scroll-snap-stop: always;      
      overflow-anchor: none;
      overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
      padding-inline-start: {{ section.settings.mobile_inset }}px;
      scroll-padding-inline-start: {{ section.settings.mobile_inset }}px;
      padding-inline-end: 0;
    }

    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__media {
      scroll-snap-align: start;
      scroll-snap-stop: always;
    }

    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__media:last-child {
      margin-inline-end: {{ section.settings.mobile_inset }}px;
    }

    {%- comment -%}
      Arrow treatment from the homepage carousels: 38px charcoal disc, white
      chevron, 2px white ring, no shadow. The theme's own circle-button
      component is reused - only its colours and size are reassigned, so hover,
      focus and disabled behaviour stay native.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__carousel-with-arrows .circle-button {
      --circle-button-size: 2.375rem;
      --circle-button-background: 43 39 36;
      --circle-button-text-color: 255 255 255;
      box-sizing: border-box;
      background: {{ section.settings.arrow_background }};
      color: {{ section.settings.arrow_color }};
      border: 2px solid {{ section.settings.arrow_border }};
      box-shadow: none;
    }

    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__carousel-with-arrows .circle-button > svg {
      width: 0.875rem;
      stroke-width: 1.75;
      stroke-linecap: round;
      stroke-linejoin: round;
    }

    {%- comment -%}
      Arrows over the media, under the "thumbnails" control. The buttons ship
      as bare tap-areas - Prestige styles them only inside its own
      carousel-with-arrows wrapper, which thumbnails mode deliberately does not
      use. They are given the homepage carousel's disc here: 38px charcoal
      circle, white chevron, 2px white ring.

      The carousel is the positioning context, so the buttons sit over the
      media rather than beside it, and z-index keeps them above the image
      without being clipped - the track scrolls but does not hide them.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__carousel-with-thumb-arrows {
      position: relative;
    }

    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__carousel-with-thumb-arrows > :is(carousel-prev-button, carousel-next-button) {
      display: contents;
    }

    {%- comment -%}
      Size and vertical position come from settings via custom properties, so
      the chevron scales with the disc from one value rather than two that can
      drift apart. Position is a percentage of the CAROUSEL, which is the
      containing block - so it tracks the media area and never the thumbnail
      strip or the viewport.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__carousel-with-thumb-arrows {
      --pdp-arrow-size: {{ section.settings.arrow_size }}px;
      --pdp-arrow-top: {{ section.settings.arrow_position }}%;
    }

    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__carousel-with-thumb-arrows :is(carousel-prev-button, carousel-next-button) button {
      z-index: 2;
      position: absolute;
      inset-block-start: var(--pdp-arrow-top);
      transform: translateY(-50%);
      display: grid;
      place-items: center;
      box-sizing: border-box;
      width: var(--pdp-arrow-size);
      height: var(--pdp-arrow-size);
      border: 2px solid {{ section.settings.arrow_border }};
      border-radius: 50%;
      background: {{ section.settings.arrow_background }};
      color: {{ section.settings.arrow_color }};
      box-shadow: none;
    }

    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__carousel-with-thumb-arrows carousel-prev-button button {
      inset-inline-start: {{ section.settings.arrow_inset }}px;
    }

    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__carousel-with-thumb-arrows carousel-next-button button {
      inset-inline-end: {{ section.settings.arrow_inset }}px;
    }

    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__carousel-with-thumb-arrows :is(carousel-prev-button, carousel-next-button) button > svg {
      /* 14/38 of the disc - the homepage proportion, kept as the button resizes */
      width: calc(var(--pdp-arrow-size) * 0.368);
      stroke-width: 1.75;
      stroke-linecap: round;
      stroke-linejoin: round;
    }

    /* Arrow visibility follows the carousel's own state. ScrollCarousel sets
       and clears the disabled attribute on the prev/next buttons as the
       position changes, so display: none on a disabled button gives exactly
       the required sequence with no JavaScript and no index tracking of our
       own:

         first media   prev disabled -> hidden, next visible
         middle        neither disabled -> both visible
         last media    next disabled -> hidden, prev visible
         one media     both disabled -> both hidden

       display, not opacity or visibility: the button is removed from the box
       entirely rather than left transparent. The buttons are absolutely
       positioned, so nothing reserves space for a hidden one and the media
       does not shift.

       Because the state is the carousel's, every navigation route updates it -
       swipe, arrow click and thumbnail click all move the same carousel, so
       the arrows and the active thumbnail can never disagree. */
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__carousel-with-thumb-arrows :is(carousel-prev-button, carousel-next-button) button[disabled] {
      display: none;
    }

    {%- comment -%}
      theme.css forces each item to 45vw and centres it when arrows are on,
      which is what stops the Figma's full-width item with a sliver of the
      next. Reset to the grid width above. This is an override under this
      section's class, not an edit to theme.css.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__carousel-with-arrows .product-gallery__media {
      width: auto;
      margin-inline: 0;
    }

    .shopify-section--main-product .product-gallery__carousel-with-arrows .product-gallery__media:last-child {
      margin-inline-end: {{ section.settings.mobile_inset }}px;
    }
  }
</style>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "PDP gallery presentation",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "shopify-section--pdp-gallery-css",
  "enabled_on": {
    "templates": ["product"]
  },
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Renders nothing. Adjusts the product gallery's presentation only, and finds the product section automatically. Set the product section's Desktop media layout to \"Grid (2 per row)\" and Mobile controls to \"Arrows\" for this to apply."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Product information"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "info_width",
      "label": "Information column width (desktop)",
      "info": "The gallery takes the remaining width.",
      "min": 487,
      "max": 587,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 537
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "info_gap",
      "label": "Gap between gallery and information",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 80,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 32
    },
    {
